    "markdown": "---\ntitle: \"\"\nformat:\n  html:\n    css: IndexStyle_v1.css\n    page-layout: full\n    toc: true\n    toc-location: right\n    \n\n# We keep the listing definition here for the gallery section\nlisting:\n  categories: true\n  contents: \n    - \"1. Linear Regression for continuous variable.qmd\"\n    - \"2. Multiple continuous variables.qmd\"\n    - \"3. Interaction between continuous variables.qmd\"\n    - \"4. Categorical variable.qmd\"\n    - \"5. Binomial model.qmd\"\n    - \"6. Beta binomial model.qmd\"\n    - \"7. Poisson model.qmd\"\n    - \"8. Poisson mode with offset.qmd\"\n    - \"9. Gamma-Poisson.qmd\"\n    - \"10. Categorical model.qmd\"\n    - \"11. Multinomial model.qmd\"\n    - \"12. Dirichlet model.qmd\"\n    - \"13. Zero inflated.qmd\"\n    - \"14. Survival analysis.qmd\"\n    - \"15. Varying intercepts.qmd\"\n    - \"16. Varying slopes.qmd\"\n    - \"17. Nested varying effects.qmd\"\n    - \"18. Phylogenetic analysis.qmd\"\n    - \"19. Gaussian processes.qmd\"\n    - \"20. Measuring error.qmd\"\n    - \"21. Missing data.qmd\"\n    - \"22. Latent variable.qmd\"\n    - \"23. PCA.qmd\"\n    - \"24. GMM.qmd\"\n    - \"25. DPMM.qmd\"\n    - \"26. Network model.qmd\"\n    - \"27. Network with block model.qmd\"\n    - \"28. Network control for data collection biases.qmd\"\n    - \"29. Network Edges Inference.qmd\"\n    - \"30. Network Metrics.qmd\"\n    - \"31. Network-Based Diffusion Analysis.qmd\"\n    - \"32. EWA.qmd\"\n    - \"33. BNN.qmd\"\n    - \"34. BNN for classification.qmd\"\n    - \"35. BNN for multiclass classification.qmd\"\n    - \"36. BNNC unsupervised.qmd\"\n\n  type: grid\n  grid-columns: 3\n  sort: \"order asc\"\n  page-size: 40\n  fields: [image, title, reading-time, categories, description]\n  image-height: \"250\"\n---\n\n::: {.panel .welcome-panel}\n\n# Welcome to BayesForge (BF)\n\n## An open-source library for Python, R and Julia\n\n<div class=\"container\">\n\n  <ul style=\"text-align: left; list-style-position: inside;\">\n  <strong class=\"text-primary\">Scalable</strong> - Built on top of Numpyro, TensorFlow Probability and Jax, for cpu, gpu and tpu   vectorization and parallelization.  \n\n  <strong class=\"text-primary\">Flexibility trade-offs</strong> - low-level abstraction coding available but also pre-build function for  high-level of abstraction. \n\n  <strong class=\"text-primary\">Unified</strong> - One framework for both Python and R.\n\n  <strong class=\"text-primary\">Accessibility</strong> - 30 documented models.\n\n  <strong class=\"text-primary\">Intuitive</strong> - model-building syntax.\n\n  </ul>\n\n<h3>\n  <a class=\"btn-get-started\" href=\"start/Installation.html\">Get Started</a>\n</h3>\n</div>\n\n::: \n\n::: {.panel .welcome-show}\n<div class=\"container\">\n## One Mental Model. Three Languages.\n\n**BF** provides a unified experience across Julia, Python, and R. Whether you work in R's formula syntax, Python's object-oriented approach, or Julia's mathematical elegance, the model logic remains consistent.\n\n\n### Compare the Syntax\n\n\n::: {.panel-tabset}\n### Python Syntax\n```python\ndef model(height, weight):\n    # Priors\n    sigma = bf.dist.uniform(0, 50, name='sigma', shape=(1,))\n    alpha = bf.dist.normal(178, 20, name='alpha', shape=(1,))\n    beta  = bf.dist.normal(0, 1, name='beta', shape=(1,))\n\n    # Likelihood\n    mu = alpha + beta * weight\n    bf.dist.normal(mu, sigma, obs=height)\n```\n\n### R Syntax\n```r\nmodel <- function(height, weight){\n  # Priors\n  sigma = bf.dist.uniform(0, 50, name='sigma', shape=c(1))\n  alpha = bf.dist.normal(178, 20, name='alpha', shape=c(1))\n  beta  = bf.dist.normal(0, 1, name='beta', shape=c(1))\n\n  # Likelihood\n  mu = alpha + beta * weight\n  bf.dist.normal(mu, sigma, obs=height)\n}\n```\n\n### Julia Syntax\n```Julia\n@BF function model(weight, height)\n    # Priors\n    sigma = bf.dist.uniform(0, 50, name='sigma', shape=(1,))\n    alpha = bf.dist.normal(178, 20, name='alpha', shape=(1,))\n    beta  = bf.dist.normal(0, 1, name='beta', shape=(1,))\n\n    # Likelihood\n    mu = alpha + beta * weight\n    bf.dist.normal(mu, sigma, obs=height)\nend\n```\n:::\n</div>\n:::\n\n\n::: {.panel .welcome-show}\n\n<div class=\"container\">\n## A Unified, Scalable, and Accessible Framework\n\n::: {.panel-tabset}\n### Pipeline\n```python\nfrom BayesForge import bf\n\n# Setup device------------------------------------------------\nm = bf(platform='cpu') # cpu, gpu or tpu\n\n# Import Data ------------------------------------------------\nm.data(data_path) \n\n# Define model ------------------------------------------------\ndef model(arg1,argb2):\n   pass\n\n# Run mcmc ------------------------------------------------\nm.fit(model) \n\n# Summary ------------------------------------------------\nm.summary()\n\n# Diagnostics ------------------------------------------------\nm.diag()\n```\n\n### Full Model definition\n```python\n\ndef model(kcal_per_g, index_clade):\n    alpha = m.bf.dist.normal(0, 0.5, shape=(4,), name = 'a') # shape based on the number of clades\n    beta = m.bf.dist.normal(0, 0.5, shape=(4,), name = 'b')\n    sigma = m.bf.dist.exponential( 1, name = 's')    \n    mu = a[index_clade]+b[index_clade]*mass\n    m.normal(mu, s, obs=kcal_per_g)\n```\n\n### Prebuilt function\n```python\ndef model(kcal_per_g, index_clade):\n    a = m.dist.normal(5, 2,  name = 'a')\n    b = m.dist.normal(-1, 0.5, name = 'b')\n    sigma = m.dist.exponential( 1,  name = 'sigma')\n\n    varying_intercept, varying_slope = m.effects.varying_effects(\n      N_group = N_cafes,\n      group = cafe,\n      global_intercept= a,\n      global_slope= b,\n      group_name = 'cafe'\n    )\n```\n\n### Model to Latex\n```python\nfrom BayesForge import bf\nm = bf(platform='cpu')\n\n# define model ------------------------------------------------\ndef model(weight, height):    \n    alpha = m.dist.normal( 178, 20, name = 'a')\n    beta = m.dist.log_normal( 0, 1, name = 'b')   \n    sigma = m.dist.uniform( 0, 50, name = 's')\n    m.dist.normal(alpha + beta * weight , sigma, obs=height)\n\n# Run sampler ------------------------------------------------\nm.model = model\nm.latex()\n```\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nheight &\\sim \\text{Normal}(\\alpha + \\beta * weight, \\sigma) \\\\\n\\sigma &\\sim \\text{Uniform}(0, 50) \\\\\n\\beta &\\sim \\text{LogNormal}(0, 1) \\\\\n\\alpha &\\sim \\text{Normal}(178, 20)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n<!-- Only ONE of {Network Exploration, Distributions visualization} can be a\n     tab: each emits a large raw-HTML output (the NetExplorer <iframe>, the\n     Plotly figure) that terminates the panel-tabset and drops every tab after\n     it. Network Exploration is kept as the last tab; Distributions\n     visualization follows as a section right after the tab-set. -->\n\n\n### Distributions visualization\n\n::: {#dist-visualization .cell results='hold' execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nfrom BayesForge import bf\nm = bf()\nm.dist.normal( 0, 1, name = 'a', shape=(100,2, 4), sample = True, to_jax = False).hist()\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stderr}\n```\nAn NVIDIA GPU may be present on this machine, but a CUDA-enabled jaxlib is not installed. Falling back to cpu.\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}\n```\nbf v 0.0.60 package loaded\njax.local_device_count 16\n```\n:::\n\n::: {#dist-visualization-1 .cell-output .cell-output-display}\n```{=html}\n        <script>\n        window.PlotlyConfig = {MathJaxConfig: 'local'};\n        if (window.MathJax && window.MathJax.Hub && window.MathJax.Hub.Config) {window.MathJax.Hub.Config({SVG: {font: \"STIX-Web\"}});}\n        </script>\n        <script type=\"module\">import \"https://cdn.plot.ly/plotly-4.1.1.min\"</script>\n        \n```\n:::\n\n::: {#dist-visualization-2 .cell-output .cell-output-display}\n```{=html}\n<div style=\"height:525px; width:100%;\">            <script src=\"https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/mathjax/3.2.2/es5/tex-svg.min.js\"></script